  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 439 AM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\n===== Friday through the Weekend =====\n\nEnd of the week will start to signal the end of the ongoing \ndominance of the strong upper ridging that provided the hot \ntemperatures this week. An upper low over eastern Canada will start \nto deepen southward into the northeast CONUS and Canadian Maritimes. \nThis will allow the upper ridge to weaken slightly as it flattens \nand retrogrades back to the west. \n\nAs this takes place a weak backdoor sfc cold front is expected to \ndrop south-south west out of Ohio and central IN. Along with this \nboundary we will see a shift in upper winds out of the northwest. \nThis will allow for MCS that form along the edge of the departing \nridge to move from the Great Lakes southeast into the Ohio Valley. \nSPC days 2 and 3 have parts of the area under a marginal risk for \nsevere weather, this includes Friday and Saturday. While we will see \nan increase in moisture ahead of the approaching boundary on Friday \nwith continued heating taking place across the area any activity we \nget will generally be more isolated with the better chance of \nshowers and storms late Friday into Saturday. While we lack much in \nthe way of deep layer sheer, we make up for it with steep lapse \nrates and high instability. Think the main threat with any potential \nstorms that for Friday into Saturday will be strong gusty winds. \n\nAs for the heat, the Heat Advisory continues until Friday evening \nand a lot of how hot it gets will come down to the placement of the \naforementioned backdoor cold front and how much convective \ndevelopment we may get in the afternoon. As of now, the current \nforecast still see the potential for highs to be in the mid to upper \n90s on Friday with heat index values above 100 and a Major to \nExtreme HeatRisk across the area on Friday. It remains hot for \nSaturday with high ranging from the low/mid 90s from the Bluegrass \nover to I-65 with mid/upper 90s west of the I-65 corridor. The Heat \nadvisory may need to be extended for parts of the area going into \nthe weekend. By Saturday the front will be working across the \nCommonwealth and we could still be dealing with more scattered \nshowers and storms with the threat of gusty winds. Things look more \ndry and more seasonable when it comes to the heat as we go into the \nrest of the long holiday weekend. \n \n\n===== Next Week =====\n\nDry weather is expected for Labor Day and into the first half of \nnext week as the upper ridge begins to shift east with time again. \nTemps are forecast to be in the lower 90s next week with dewpoints \nin the upper 60s.",
